While Affinity is used to attract pods to specific nodes, Taints and Tolerations are used to repel pods from nodes:

Taint (applied to a Node): "Keep away unless you have a pass."

Example: A node with an expensive GPU is tainted so standard web apps don't accidentally schedule on it and consume resources.

Toleration (applied to a Pod): "I have a pass for that taint."

The toleration does not force the pod onto that node; it simply allows the scheduler to place it there if needed.


Common Taint Effects:

NoSchedule: If a pod doesn't have a matching toleration, it cannot be scheduled on this node.

PreferNoSchedule: The scheduler tries to avoid placing untolerating pods here, but will if no other nodes are available.

NoExecute: If a running pod on the node lacks this toleration, it is immediately evicted/killed off the node.

